> ### OPEN -- filed 2026-08-31: the warn-once latches that are FUNCTION-STATIC, so one instance
> ### silences every other
>
> **`staticlatchscope`** -- a `static` inside a function is process-global, not per-instance.
> Every one of these is a warn-once latch on a **guest-drivable** diagnostic, so one guest
> access to one device instance permanently silences that diagnostic for every other instance
> of the same device in the machine, and for every later machine in the same process.
>
> **DERIVER -- BY SCOPE, NOT BY NAME.  Three name-based censuses of this class have now come
> back wrong, in both directions, and the third was the author's own using the deriver he had
> just written telling other people not to transcribe numbers:**
>
>     # every function-scope static int in a device file (indented, or inline after a brace)
>     grep -rc -e '^[[:space:]]\+static int ' -e '{[[:space:]]*static int ' src/devices/*.c \
>         | grep -v ':0$'
>
> **On 2026-08-31 that gives 30 sites across 13 files, of which 28 across 11 files are
> warn-once latches.**  The two exclusions are counters rather than latches and must stay
> excluded: `dev_algor.c` (`static int x; if (((++x) & 0xffff) == 0)`, a periodic RTC-bit
> hack) and `dev_mc146818.c` (`static int x; if (++x == 1)`).  Largest populations:
> `dev_osiop.c` 8, `dev_pcc2.c` 5, `dev_dec21143.c`/`dev_disk.c`/`dev_jazz.c`/`dev_pvr.c`/
> `dev_sgi_re.c`/`dev_vga.c` 2 each.
>
> **WHY THE NAME PATTERNS ALL FAILED, since this is the reusable part:** they keyed on the
> variable being called `w`/`warned`/`first`, and the population's names are
> `warned` 13, `w` 6, `first` 2, plus singletons `warned2`, `warn_fs`, `warn`, `w_warned`,
> `scc_oob_warned`, `r_warned`.  **A latch is defined by its SCOPE and its use, not by its
> spelling**, so a name pattern cannot enumerate this class and every attempt undercounted.
>
> *(Demoted literals, kept only as the record of what was measured and never as a current
> claim: `10 sites / 3 files` as filed at `cae3dc8`; `23 / 7` in a checkpoint correction the
> same day, which wrongly gave `dev_sgi_re.c` 4 -- it was 2 before `#499` and 2 after, so that
> figure was never right and was not this project's own edits moving it; `26 / 10` from a
> widened name pattern hours later.  All three are superseded by the scope deriver above.)*
>
> **THE INTENT IS RIGHT EVERYWHERE AND ONLY THE SCOPE IS WRONG.**  `#179`'s own comment says
> *"guest-reachable; ignore the write instead of `fatal()`-flooding host stdout"*; `#119`'s
> says *"was `exit(1)` -> warn-once + continue"*.  Both are correct de-fatalisations this
> project would make again.  **This row is about SCOPE and must not be read as reopening
> either round.**  The house form is a per-instance bit in the device's own struct
> (`d->warned` plus `(once)` in the text), which `#337`, `#460`, `#461`, `#497` and `#498`
> all use.
>
> **HOW IT WAS FOUND, and it is the transferable part: TWO CENSUSES WERE BOTH SHORT, IN
> OPPOSITE WAYS.**  A measuring seat censused it while answering an unrelated question and
> reported *"4 lines, all in this file, the tree's only such sites"* -- true of its pattern
> (`static int first` / `_used[`) and false of the class, because two rounds spell the latch
> `static int w = 0`.  Then a packet-fed seat on a DIFFERENT row challenged an `exit(` regex
> of mine as too narrow; **the count it challenged did not move, but chasing the four extra
> hits surfaced `#119`'s comments and hence the second file.**  Neither seat was looking for
> this class.  *A pattern-based census is only ever a census of its pattern, and the way out
> was two independent narrow greps disagreeing.*
>
> **SCOPE NOTE:** `dev_jazz.c` is one of the six paths in `tools/est_divergence.txt`, so a
> change there is SEC-only and owes no `est/` counterpart -- unlike the other two files.
>
> **CONTROL a fix owes:** a machine with TWO instances of the device, expecting two messages
> where the current code emits one.  That is what distinguishes the repair from a no-op
> rename.

> ### OPEN -- filed 2026-08-31: `SGIRE_W_*` spells its bits as literal `1 << N`
>
> **`sgirelatchenum`** -- a duplicate enumerator is legal C and silent, and `dev_sgi_re.c`'s
> two devices cast the same `struct sgi_re_data *`, so `d->warned` is SHARED.  A collision
> makes one arm's decline permanently suppressible by unrelated traffic to the other.
>
> **Three instances in three days:**
>
> 1. **MEASURED.**  `#498` added `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN = 1 << 6`; `#499`'s witness generator
>    independently inserted `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR = 1 << 6`.  **14/14 total escape**, with a
>    transcript showing one guest `sb` to `sgi_de` permanently silencing `sgi_re`'s catch-all.
> 2. The codex seat independently proposed `1 << 7` -> `1 << 6` as the canonical clause-(d)
>    mutant for the `#500` fix -- an adversary reaches for this collision unprompted.
> 3. **PREVENTED.**  `#499` and `#500` were both designed for `1 << 7` while in flight
>    together.  Caught by listing the enum before writing either fix; `#500` moves to `1 << 9`.
>
> *Two of three were found by measurement AFTER the fact; the third by one `grep` BEFORE it.*
>
> **THE FIX is the idiom this project already blesses.**  `SH4_VAL_*` was converted to a C99
> enum ending in `_NCLASS` precisely so duplicates are impossible by construction, and the
> carrier marks that work DISCHARGED.  Same shape here with one extra step, since these are
> MASKS not ids -- enumerate the BIT POSITIONS and derive the masks:
> `enum { SGIRE_WB_TLBA, ..., SGIRE_WB_NBITS };` then
> `#define SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN (1 << SGIRE_WB_TLBA)`.  The enumerator auto-increments, so two
> bits cannot collide however many rounds add one, and `SGIRE_WB_NBITS` gives a compile-time
> check that the set still fits `uint32_t warned`.
>

