NOTE   *** THE DESIGN IS DONE, THE TITLE'S COUNT IS STALE, AND AN ACCEPT ROUND WOULD RED THE GATE UNLESS ONE PROBE ROW MOVES FIRST.  codex seat, 2026-08-30, farmed read-only while agy was quota-walled; every load-bearing claim re-checked here. ***  **THE COUNT: THREE NUMBERS ARE IN CIRCULATION AND ALL THREE ARE RIGHT ABOUT DIFFERENT THINGS.**  A comment-aware census of `dev_footbridge.c` finds **10** live `exit(` calls.  **TWO of them are in `DEVINIT(footbridge)`** -- `footbridge unimpl machine type` and `footbridge: unimplemented machine type.` -- which are CONSTRUCTION-TIME and not guest-reachable, because no guest chooses its own machine type.  That leaves **EIGHT guest-access kills**, and this row's title says NINE.  The ninth was the divide-by-zero-on-a-guest-written-timer-encoding fixed by `#432` (`35302f2`), verified by `git log -S`.  **L4: the title is stale and is left unedited on purpose -- titles are identity strings other records grep -- but no future round may quote the nine.**  **TWO DISPOSITIONS, THREE ROUND-GROUPS.**  Not one round, and the split is by semantic class rather than by convenience:  (1) **ACCEPT, one site**: `read from ENABLE SET?` at `+0x188`.  Table 7-10 (`dc21285.txt:6821`) and the IRQEnable prose (`:6911`) make it a documented READ whose value can only be changed via EnableSet/EnableClear -- and **the model already computes `d->irq_enable` and then discards it by dying**.  So: delete the kill, KEEP the value, no latch and no message, because there is no event to announce.  This is the FOURTH option-set member, and a bounded-announce here would ship a wrong record in product code.  (2) **DECLINE + BOUNDED ANNOUNCE, five sites, one class**: the invalid-DIRECTION interrupt accesses -- WRITE to IRQStatus `+0x180` and FIQStatus `+0x280` (read-only, `:6886`), WRITE to IRQRawStatus `+0x184` and FIQRawStatus `+0x284` (read-only, `:6901`), and READ from ENABLE CLEAR `+0x18c` (write-only).  **`+0x18c` carries an extra obligation the others do not: the decline must NOT expose the currently assigned `d->irq_enable`**, which is the value a naive 'just return the field' fix would leak.  (3) **DECLINE + an ACCURATE announce, two sites, a DIFFERENT rationale**: the SDRAM Base Address Mask `+0x100` (`:5844`) and Offset `+0x104` (`:5897`) reads.  These registers are R/W and legal -- **the model simply does not implement them** -- so the message must say `unimplemented read`, NOT `invalid access`.  Getting that wording wrong is the same wrong-record defect as (1), one step milder.  *** THE OPERATIONAL TRAP, AND IT IS THE SHARPEST THING IN THIS FINDING: `gate_arm.sh` REQUIRES THE `+0x188` KILL TO KEEP HAPPENING. ***  Its row is `check "footbridge: the one-instruction kill arm still dies" ... '^K1 .*ok$' ... 1`, and `K1` is precisely the ENABLE-SET read the ACCEPT round would fix.  **So the ACCEPT round turns a GREEN GATE RED by succeeding**, and the fix is to REPOINT K1 at `+0x18c` in the same commit.  A round that does not know this reads the red as a regression in its own work.  Verified by opening the gate.  **AND THE PROBE'S FAILABILITY LEG RESTS ON A PRODUCT DEFECT**, which is fine until it is the LAST one: `footbridge_sites_probe.py` reaches exactly one fatal site (K1) and uses its death as the proof the guest code got that far.  Once the final live kill is repaired there is no product defect left to point at, and the leg must become a MUTATION/failability leg instead.  That is a real cost of finishing this cluster and it should be planned, not discovered.  DETECTOR DEBT the design already owes: seed `irq_enable` with a NONZERO write to `+0x188` before reading it, so the ACCEPT row cannot pass on the reset zero; a row per remaining live site checking survival, accurate bounded reporting AND no state mutation; and for `+0x18c`, that the invalid read neither clears `irq_enable` nor returns it.  **A PER-SITE LATCH BITMASK, not one shared latch** -- otherwise the first access kind to fire suppresses the first useful report for every other, which is `#490`'s measured `M3shared` shape.  *Provenance and its limit: the datasheet citations are `dc21285.txt` line anchors from a seat that read the file.  I verified the count reconciliation, the `#432` attribution and the gate coupling myself; I did NOT re-read all six datasheet sections, and this row says so rather than implying a completeness it does not have.*  *** TWO FARMED RUNS OF THE SAME SEAT ON THE SAME BRIEF AGREED ON EVERYTHING CHECKABLE AND SPLIT ON ONE DESIGN CALL.  Both are recorded; neither is picked, because picking is what the round is for. ***  (Both ran because a `${j}` in the launch command did not expand, so each job read all three briefs -- an accident that bought an unplanned replication.)  **AGREED, and these are the facts:** eight guest-access kills not nine, the ninth removed by `#432`; `+0x188` is ACCEPT; the four writes to read-only status registers are decline+announce; and WIDTH vs DIRECTION at the sgi_re sites are TWO rounds, not one, because they share an address and a probe but not a semantic class.  **SPLIT 1 -- how many round-groups.**  Run A said THREE (accept / five invalid-direction / two SDRAM).  Run B said FOUR, splitting `+0x18c` (IRQEnableClear, a READ of a write-only register) into its own group because it carries an obligation the other four do not: **the present code must also STOP EXPOSING `d->irq_enable`**, which a plain decline does not accomplish.  Run A recorded that obligation but kept the site in the same group.  **Run B's split is the better one on the round-scope rule's own text** -- a site needing a state-suppression the others do not is not covered by the same fix design -- but it is a judgement call and it is recorded as one.  *** SPLIT 2, AND IT PROPOSES A FIFTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: `IMPLEMENT`. ***  For the two SDRAM Base Address registers (`+0x100` mask, `+0x104` offset) Run A said decline + an ACCURATE announce (*'unimplemented read'*, not *'invalid access'*).  Run B said flatly **'none of the four offered options fits'** and proposed implementing them: reset-zero state with masked write/readback.  **The disagreement is real because these registers are LEGAL AND READ/WRITE.**  The existing four members all presume the access is either unserviceable (delete / decline / decline+announce) or already correctly computed (ACCEPT).  A legal R/W register the model simply never implemented is neither: declining it announces truthfully and still returns a wrong answer to a guest that wrote then read back.  **THE ADDITION LICENCE PUTS THE BURDEN ON `IMPLEMENT`, and that is the right way round:** additions to product code here are licensed by a MEASURED DELTA or a TRANSFERRED WARRANT, never by 'it cannot hurt'.  Implementing two registers is an addition, so it owes one of those -- a guest that actually reads them back, or an in-tree precedent for modelling an SDRAM sizing register.  **Until one exists, decline+accurate-announce is the licensed choice and IMPLEMENT is the filed alternative.**  A round taking these two sites must answer this explicitly rather than inheriting whichever note it reads first.  *Recorded now because the four-member option set is carrier doctrine, and a proposed FIFTH member is a rule question -- which this project's own splitting rule says goes to the adjudicating seat at the next round boundary rather than being settled in passing.*  *** THE K1 GATE DEPENDENCY IS VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY, NOT TAKEN ON THE DESIGN'S WORD.  Main loop, 2026-08-30. ***  `gate_arm.sh` carries `check "footbridge: the one-instruction kill arm still dies"` and `footbridge_sites_probe.py` emits `K1  kill arm dies at 0x188, exit 1`.  **So the gate genuinely asserts that the kill KEEPS HAPPENING, and the ACCEPT round reddens it BY SUCCEEDING.**  A round that does not know this reads the red as a regression in its own work -- which is why it is written here rather than discovered at gate time.  **AND THE DEEPER PROBLEM IS THE ONE WORTH CARRYING FORWARD: the probe's failability leg RESTS ON A PRODUCT DEFECT.**  It reaches exactly one fatal site and uses that death as proof the guest code got that far.  That is sound while other kills remain and self-defeating when the round's whole purpose is to remove the last one.  *A test that proves its own liveness by reaching a crash is spending a defect as apparatus, and the bill comes due on the round that fixes it.*  **HOLD QUESTION SET 2026-08-30.**  This row was held with `hold_question: None` -- the exact defect `check_stage_panels.py` exists to catch, and it passed only because the hold predates the checker's cutoff.  *A stage held for a seat must carry the question it is held for; a seat fired against a disposition note has to GUESS what it is being asked, and a guessed question yields an answer to the wrong question that still looks like a discharged stage.*  *** THE HOLD QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER MOVED THE ROUND'S SHAPE IN FOUR PLACES.  Adjudicated 2026-08-30; every load-bearing claim re-verified here by grep before recording. ***  **(1) THE REPOINT IS LEGITIMATE -- BUT NOT FOR THE REASON I ASKED ABOUT.**  I framed it as an oracle-correction: a shipped row pinning the opposite of the architecture.  **K1 pins nothing about `+0x188`'s correct behaviour.**  Its own header says so -- `+0x188` is *an UNFIXED site, used here rather than repaired, because a probe that reports SURVIVED for every row is vacuous unless something in it can still die*.  It is a **FAILABILITY CONTROL whose CONSUMABLE the fix consumes**, and retargeting a control to another live consumable is not re-pinning an oracle.  **THE ORDER DISCIPLINE TRANSFERS ANYWAY, and this is the laundering hazard that IS real:** a round that repointed the arms and shipped nothing else would change behaviour with **zero rows changing colour** -- no red ever measured, observationally identical to fitting the harness around the fix.  So on the committed PRE-FIX build, before any `exit(1)` is touched: the repointed arms must still DIE at the new site, and the new ACCEPT row must be RED **for the reason under test** (its fatals carrying the ENABLE SET text -- mechanical rule 4).  A dead replacement control is the check-that-cannot-fail shape.  *** (2) THE BLAST RADIUS IS UNDERCOUNTED BY A FACTOR OF THREE, AND IT CARRIES A THREE-WEEK FUSE.  VERIFIED BY GREP. ***  The design says *repoint K1*.  **THREE committed probes plant `0xE5907188`** -- `footbridge_sites_probe.py:103`, `fbpending_drain_probe.py:226`, `fbpending_witness.py:195` -- and the latter two score their K rows on `HOST-DIED` (`fbpending_drain_probe.py:659`).  **`fbpending_drain_probe.py` is dated-exempt from wiring only until 2026-09-20**, with `gate_arm.sh` named as its home -- so a round that repoints one arm leaves two probes broken on disk and a fuse that wires one into a gate where its K row reads as a regression.  **All three move in the same commit**, each with its own pre-fix still-dies measurement.  **(3) THE THIRD ORACLE OBLIGATION BITES, AND THIS ROW'S OWN PRESCRIPTION IS INSUFFICIENT.**  The repointed arm observes the SAME CHANNEL RELOCATED -- guest read, fatal text, exit -- so without more the FIXED site would carry no row at all, which is worse than a row that observes the same value either way.  The other-channel row is the ACCEPT row, and this row previously prescribed *seed with a nonzero write, then read it back*.  **That is RAM-INDISTINGUISHABLE and the probe's own header already rejects it** -- *RAM, or any decode that merely absorbed the store, would return the value unchanged*.  **THE ROW MUST CARRY THE HANDLER'S OWN TRANSFORMATION AS ITS SIGNATURE**, and the set/clear machinery supplies one free: write V1 then V2 to `+0x188` and read back **V1|V2** (the `|=` accumulation), or write V1 to `+0x188` and V2 to `+0x18c` and read back **V1 & ~V2**.  *Any absorbing decode returns the last write; only this device returns the accumulation.*  **(4) A STRUCTURAL CAUTION THE DESIGN MISSED: the accept sequence needs its OWN probe arm, not the shared `BODY` list.**  `BODY` is executed by arms T, L and K alike, so a `+0x188` read placed there makes PRE-FIX arm L die mid-run, zeroing the liveness rows and mis-attributing the red -- *failed for some other reason*, which is the guard mechanical rule 4 exists to enforce.  A fourth launch costs about 0.6 s against a measured 1.9 s wall.  **(5) REPOINT TO `+0x100`, NOT `+0x18c` -- one hop instead of two.**  `+0x18c` is consumed by the DECLINE round under this row's own group order, so targeting it means three arms moved now and three moved AGAIN.  `+0x100` (`ldr r7,[r0,#256]`, fatal `read from sdram_ba_mask`) is a last-group site: same one-word edit, and the pre-fix measurement doubles as a rung-3 witness the SDRAM round inherits.  *** (6) THE TITLE IS DE-COUNTED, AND THE PREMISE FOR LEAVING IT WAS MEASURED FALSE. ***  The design left `nine` standing because *titles are identity strings other records grep*.  **Swept: the title text appears NOWHERE outside `ledger.json` itself**, the grep identity is the `id` field, and `gen_dashboard.py` uses the title for display only while `check_stage_panels`, `check_seats_read`, `gen_codex_wall` and `check_fable_queue` never read titles at all.  Editing it breaks zero cross-references; leaving it kept a wrong count in the row's most-read line -- **the verdict-line-position problem, where the half that survives truncation is the wrong half.**  **De-counted rather than re-counted to EIGHT**, following this repo's own adjudicated form: *removed rather than re-counted -- a prose list that duplicates a generated table has no way to stay true*.  The `n6` id is untouched, and the note keeps the NINE verbatim with its `#432` warrant, so provenance survives.  **THREE STALE CITATIONS TO FIX IN THE SAME ROUND**, all naming the kill site: the probe header and `gate_arm.sh` both say `dev_footbridge.c:495-497`, and `fbpending_witness.py` says `:486-499`; the real arm is `:577-591`.  **And the anchor itself is DELETED by this fix**, so the rewritten comments must anchor on the new site's text.  *** THE 2026-09-20 FUSE IS BOUNDED TO ONE PROBE.  Swept 2026-08-30 after this row surfaced the entanglement, because the obvious next question is whether OTHER exemptions carry the same trap. ***  **All six dated exemptions expire on the same day, 2026-09-20**, and expiry means the probe gets wired into a gate.  A probe whose row depends on a site DYING then reads as a regression if the round that fixes that site has shipped -- which is exactly what this row found for `fbpending_drain_probe.py`.  **Swept all six for any dependence on a site dying** (`HOST-DIED`, a `died` flag, an `exit == 1` expectation, `s.dead`), with denominators and a positive control so a pattern-matched-nothing result could not masquerade as a clean one:      fbpending_drain_probe    14 of 727 lines   <- the known entanglement      hpcmips_ctor_probe        2 of 758         <- BOTH ARE COMMENTS, not code      wdc_altoffset_probe       0 of 698      wdc_altoffset_sig_probe   0 of 263      wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe  0 of 240      wdc_addtoinbuf_probe      0 of 863      CONTROL footbridge_sites  3 of 316         <- the pattern fires where it must  `hpcmips_ctor_probe`'s two hits were opened and are prose -- *the process died and it died HERE are different claims*, and a note about a core dump.  **No functional dependence.**  **SO THE FUSE IS EXACTLY ONE PROBE WIDE**, and it is already named in this row's blast radius.  Nothing else in the exemption table needs to move when these expire.  *The positive control is the part that makes this worth recording: a sweep of six files returning five zeros is precisely the shape this project has twice mistaken for a result, and the control is what separates 'nothing there' from 'pattern matched nothing'.*  *** AND THE BLAST RADIUS HAS THREE TIERS, NOT ONE NUMBER.  Swept 2026-08-30, generalising the K1 finding into the question that actually matters. ***  **THE FIRST FORMULATION WAS WRONG AND ITS ZERO WAS WEAK.**  Sweeping the GATES for rows whose label demands a death found exactly one of **423 check rows** -- the K1 row -- and a second pattern looking for `HOST-DIED` in a check's want returned **0 of 423**.  That zero proves nothing: gates consume probe OUTPUT TOKENS (`^K1 .*ok$`), so the death-dependence lives INSIDE the probe, not in the gate row.  *A denominator validates the artifact, never the pattern's fitness for its format.*  **THE RIGHT QUESTION IS: WHICH PROBES SCORE A ROW ON A DEATH, AND WHICH OF THOSE DOES A GATE ACTUALLY RUN?**  Three probes score on a death -- `fbpending_drain_probe.py` (2 sites), `footbridge_sites_probe.py` (1), `fbpending_witness.py` (1) -- and **all three are REFERENCED by gates**, which looked at first like the radius tripling again.  **IT DOES NOT: SEPARATING INVOCATIONS FROM MENTIONS LEAVES EXACTLY ONE.**  `gate_arm.sh:987` genuinely runs `footbridge_sites_probe.py`; **every other reference in `gate_arm.sh`, `gate_hygiene.sh` and `gate_offline.sh` is a COMMENT.**  That is the mentions-are-not-wiring distinction `check_probe_wiring.py` exists to enforce, applied by hand here and giving the same answer.  **SO THE THREE TIERS ARE:**  **(1) TODAY** -- one gate row reddens, `gate_arm.sh`'s K1, via the only real invocation.  **(2) ON 2026-09-20** -- `fbpending_drain_probe.py`'s exemption expires, it is wired into `gate_arm.sh`, and its K row joins the first.  **(3) SILENTLY, AT ANY TIME** -- `fbpending_witness.py`'s K row breaks on disk and **NO GATE RUNS IT**, so nothing reports the breakage at all.  *That is the worst tier despite being the least urgent: a broken detector nobody runs is indistinguishable from a working one, and it is exactly the state the wiring checker was built to make visible.*  All three arms still move in the same commit -- the tiers change WHEN each would be noticed, not whether each is broken.  *** THE DATASHEET SPLITS WHAT THIS ROW TREATS AS ONE CLASS.  Derived 2026-08-30 from `_scratchpad/dc21285.txt`, cross-confirmed in THREE independent places so no extraction stagger is possible: the register summary (`IRQEnable/IRQEnableSet 188h`, `IRQEnableClear 18Ch`), the offset table (`188h  IRQEnable  IRQEnableSet` / `18Ch  --  IRQEnableClear`), and the section heading `7.3.32 IRQEnableSet/FIQEnableSet Register--Offset 188h/288h`. ***  Constants confirmed in `src/include/thirdparty/dc21285reg.h`.  **(a) READ of 0x188 -> ACCEPT.**  *"IRQEnable/FIQEnable Register--Offset 188h/288h.  This read-only register is used to mask the interrupt input sources... The value of this register can only be changed by writing to the EnableSet and EnableClear registers.  Reset value: 0."*  The emulator ALREADY does `odata = d->irq_enable;` -- the documented value -- and then calls `exit(1)` on it.  Delete the kill, KEEP THE VALUE, **no latch**: there is no event to announce, because the read is implemented and is not ignored.  A bounded announce here would ship a wrong record in product code.  **(b) READ of 0x18c -> DECLINE.**  The offset table gives `--` for its read function: there is NO defined read.  The emulator returns `d->irq_enable`, which is a GUESS, then exits.  **Different disposition from its immediate neighbour, and the code treats them identically.**  **(c) WRITE to FIQ_STATUS (0x280) -> DECLINE.**  *"The Status and RawStatus are read-only."*  *** WHY THIS MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TRIVIA: a single splice across all three would ACCEPT an undefined read at 0x18c and DECLINE a documented one at 0x188 -- getting both neighbours wrong, in OPPOSITE directions. ***  This is the option-set rule paying out again, with the ACCEPT member in play -- and that member is visible only from the datasheet, never from the source.  *This row's title treats 'the guest-reachable kills' as ONE THING.  It is three things.*
