ENTRY {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "assess", "d": "⏸", "date": "2026-08-16", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "policy annotation, not a review", "note": "[RUN UNDER THE PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY] This stage ran with 2 of 9 seats because the carrier's rule at the time was ONE full panel per ROUND, with pass 2 restricted to codex and Opus -- written when Grok's free tier was dead and Kimi was quota-dead. The owner replaced that on 2026-08-16 with FULL PANEL ON EVERY STAGE, stop-and-ask on any seat failure. Grandfathered FORWARD-ONLY by owner decision: not re-panelled, but marked so the dashboard does not read these blanks as seat failures. Missing: codex, agy, grok, kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax."}
ENTRY {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "assess", "d": "OK", "date": "2026-08-30", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "outcome": "UPHELD", "grader": "mainloop (Fable family) -- *** SELF-GRADED, disclosed ***", "ev": "_scratchpad/ (reading-seat adjudication; blast radius re-verified by grep)", "outcome_note": "[HOLD-QUESTION-DISCHARGED]  *** SELF-GRADING DISCLOSED. ***  Every load-bearing claim re-checked before recording: three probes plant 0xE5907188, the drain probe scores its K row on HOST-DIED, its exemption expires 2026-09-20, and the title text exists nowhere outside ledger.json.  It refuted THIS ROW'S OWN accept-row prescription as RAM-indistinguishable using the probe's own header against it, which is the useful shape.", "note": "Discharged the hold question and moved the round's shape in four places -- the blast radius (1 arm -> 3), the repoint target (+0x18c -> +0x100, one hop not two), the accept row's oracle (readback -> the handler's OR-accumulation signature), and the title.  Honest about its own basis: a READING seat's adjudication, nothing built or run, and it explicitly honoured the embargo while #497's table runs."}

ROW gdromdmawidth SEV UNRATED STATE open
WITNESS none committed -- MEASURED 2026-08-23 at rung 3 via a scratchpad probe (reported, not pinned): the row is DELIBERATELY not shipped, because pinning 0x000000ff would enshrine this model's own behaviour as an oracle
NOTE   *** CITATIONS RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 AND TWO ARE STALE. ***  This row cites `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515` and `:889`; today those lines are bare comment continuation (`*` and `*  Mount:`) and hold no code at all.  The construct the row is about -- the DMA tail store that ignores `len` -- now sits beside the comment *"THEN the tail stores it into dma_reg[1].  The same alias exists at"*.  **DURABLE ANCHOR, replacing both numbers: `grep -n 'tail stores it into dma_reg' src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c`.**  A grep anchor that matches nothing is LOUD; a drifted line number is silent and still looks like a citation.  *The file was committed five times after this note was written, which is exactly the window the citation-form rule describes: a same-file line number is invalidated by the round's own later edits, and nobody re-checks an example once it is written down.*
ENTRY {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "Pre-existing upstream, untouched by #461, found by the pass-2 measuring seat while building mutants against the alias fix. NOT joined: #461's semantic class is 'unimplemented offset'; this is 'implemented offset, wrong width' -- a different fix design (read-modify-write honouring len, which raises an endianness question the default arm never has to answer) and a different oracle, so the round-scope rule makes it a separate round. *** IT IS THE SAME FAMILY AS THE ALIAS #461 DID FIX, AND #461's NEW RULE DOES NOT COVER IT: *** 'alignment alone separates unimplemented word from misaligned access to an implemented one' is true and says nothing about an ALIGNED NARROW write to an implemented offset. A future reader applying that rule here would conclude it is safe. Related to [hlen], but the OPPOSITE direction: hlen is a wide access spanning registers, this is a narrow access clobbering one. `dma seed` is one store away from being a witness."}
ENTRY {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "*** WITNESS CONSTRUCTIBLE AT RUNG 3, contrary to `witness: none`. Verified by READING the arm; flagged as a reading, since a probe run would have contended with a live gate. *** The carrier's rule is that 'no witness of either kind' is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked rather than asserted. Checked: `len` appears exactly THREE times in DEVICE_ACCESS(dreamcast_gdrom_dma), and all three are byte marshalling -- memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len) on the way in and two memory_writemax64(..., len, ...) on the way out. The store itself, at dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:889 (re-derived 2026-08-23), is `d->dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` -- a full 32-bit assignment with no width discrimination anywhere. THE WITNESS IS `dma seed` PLUS ONE STORE, exactly as the filing predicted: 32-bit store 0x11223344 at DMA+0x04, then a BYTE store of 0xff at DMA+0x04 (ALIGNED, so it matches `case 0x04` and is NOT the #461 alias), then read +0x04 back. Real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits; this model yields 0x000000ff. Ordinary guest instructions through real address decode on a committed machine -- rung 3, no new device and no machine edit, so no laundering. NO BUILD NEEDED EITHER: the defect is pre-existing upstream and untouched by #461, so it is in the current binary."}
ENTRY {"seat": "grok", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] *** FOUND THE DECISIVE PRECEDENT, AND IT IS IN THIS VERY FILE FROM THE PREVIOUS ROUND. *** dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515-522, written by #460: 'DO NOT \"EMULATE\" NARROW ACCESS INSTEAD. A pass-1 panel split on that, and it was MEASURED ACTIVELY HARMFUL: letting cmd_count step by 1 makes eleven byte writes plus one word write go 11 -> 13, SKIPPING the == 12 equality, so busy&0x08 never clears and the next write reaches the \"Too much GDROM_DATA\" exit -- otherwise unreachable. It also writes cmd[12], one past the array.' VERIFIED BY READING. Option A is exactly that shape, ruled out by the file's own measured history one round ago. Its reachability line is the right distinction too: 'Reachable as a guest instruction; unobserved as a driver path. That is not Option C.' -- rung-3 reachability is established, driver reachability is not, and the ladder says a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE while severity orders it."}
ENTRY {"seat": "agy", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Found the OTHER in-tree precedent, independently: sh4_bsc16_report() at dev_sh4.c:1646, the tree's existing handling for a narrow access to a wide register. Its conclusion: 'in-tree precedent unequivocally supports Option B (decline partial/narrow writes with a latched warn-once diagnostic)'. Two seats finding two different precedents that agree is the strongest form this panel produces -- the answer was already in the tree twice and the brief invented a three-way choice that the tree had effectively made."}
ENTRY {"seat": "codex", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Supplied the discriminating row and the comment amendment, both adopted. The row -- W32 +0x04 = 0x11223344, then W8 +0x04 = 0xff, then R32 +0x04 -- separates all three options: A gives 0x112233ff little-endian (0xff223344 big-endian), B gives 0x11223344 preserved, C gives 0x000000ff. Its amendment text is the one to use: it SEPARATES ADDRESS DECODING FROM WIDTH VALIDATION, so #461's alignment rule cannot be misread as proof that implemented cases are width-safe."}
ENTRY {"seat": "deepseek", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Reached codex's discriminating row independently, with the same three expected values including the endianness split on Option A. Its amendment wording is the crisper of the two: 'An aligned offset reaching the default arm is genuinely an unimplemented word. This does not imply that an aligned narrow access to an IMPLEMENTED offset is safe; narrow accesses to implemented registers are a separate width question and are currently not width-discriminated.'"}
ENTRY {"seat": "glm", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Option B with the clearest cost/benefit statement: one branch on `len != 4` before the tail store; consistent with #461's decline-rather-than-corrupt philosophy; establishes the missing in-tree precedent for the WIDTH axis without committing to Option A's endianness-dependent merge; and it 'turns an unreachable silent-clobber into an unreachable LOUD no-op'. Explicitly ranked B over C: 'B is C plus a guard for the same engineering cost.'"}
ENTRY {"seat": "minimax", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] *** THE ONLY SEAT THAT REFUSED TO PRETEND THE REACHABILITY QUESTION WAS SETTLED, and it is right. *** 'Probably not in well-behaved guest code, but the brief does not let us prove a negative.' It also caught a trap in the brief's own framing: adding the witness row makes the defect reachable IN CI, which 'is not the same as reachable' by a guest driver -- a distinction this project's witness ladder makes and the brief blurred. Its answer is correctly CONDITIONAL rather than confident."}
ENTRY {"seat": "kimi", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Went furthest on the endianness question the brief called the real technical risk, working out what memory_readmax64 does to a narrow value and where a byte write at +0x04 lands in dma_reg[1]. Its answer is the reason Option A is unsafe to implement from the brief alone: the fact is derivable but was NOT established in the tree, and SH-4 runs either endianness."}
ENTRY {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "*** VERDICT: OPTION C, OVERTURNING A UNANIMOUS 7-OF-7 PANEL FROM SOURCE. *** The panel converged on Option B (decline narrow writes). This seat fetched mainline Linux drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c and read it line by line: :622 `__raw_writeb(1, GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)` is the DMA TRIGGER -- a BYTE WRITE to this window's +0x18 on every transfer -- and :618 byte-READS the same offset to poll for completion, while the four value registers at :585-589 are always __raw_writel. *** SO OPTION B WOULD BREAK A BOOTING LINUX, *** and the width-blind code delivers exactly the semantics the one real narrow user needs. The defect's actual content -- clobbering a value register's upper bits -- sits in cells no known guest reaches. It also checked NetBSD (never touches the DMA window) and promemul/dreamcast.c (zero references), and was explicit that KallistiOS widths are BELIEVED only. *** AND IT REFUTED OPTION A ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN THE BRIEF EXPECTED. *** The brief called endianness the real technical risk; the seat showed it is DERIVABLE (cpu_sh.c:96 sets EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN UNCONDITIONALLY, so there is no big-endian SH in this tree and the merge is always the low lane -- the brief's 'SH-4 can run either endianness' is true of silicon and FALSE of this tree, verified independently). The real objection is an ABSENT ORACLE: 0x005f74xx is Sega's Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it -- so the filing's own 'real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits' was an UNSOURCED CLAIM, the #357/#362 shape. A would ship an invented behaviour and pin the invention with a detector row. ON PRECEDENT it corrected the panel again: the BSC16 idiom (dev_sh4.c:1620-1680) is the direct shape-m

