ENTRY {"seat": "grok", "phase": "assess", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-08-24", "note": "SPLIT OUT of gdromcondlies, which named two defects at once. Original record at OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:7575-7587, a residual of #460: once a guest reads past the buffer, COND_DATA_AVAIL stays SET while every subsequent read returns 0xffff, announced once. Pre-fix that inconsistency was unmissable because the same read ended the host -- so the de-escalation made the state defect QUIETER without removing it, which is the class de-escalation always creates. Clearing COND_DATA_AVAIL at exhaustion would repair it, and was deliberately out of scope for #460 because changing what a device ADVERTISES is a behaviour change with its own oracle. Does NOT join the BADVAL round: different class, different oracle, different fix design."}
ENTRY {"seat": "codex", "phase": "design", "d": "✓", "date": "2026-09-07", "grade": "ANSWERED", "outcome": "repin-as-PAIR;Q5-premise-refuted", "grader": "main-loop", "ev": "regress/artifacts/gdromcondavail/codex_repin_20260907.txt", "note": "UNREAD REVIEW, read in full 2026-09-07 -- 8,154 bytes, cited nowhere until now.  VERDICT: `The defect and shipped-oracle conflict are real.  Re-pin to (OVERREAD, NODATA) = (0, 1), but that alone is insufficient.  Q5's literal equality is not a surviving mutant.'  IT REFUTED ONE OF MY PREMISES AND CORRECTED THE PROPOSED ORACLE.  It also labelled its own colours as PREDICTIONS, not the committed-build RED that S5 requires -- the distinction this project's frozen-number class exists to police."}
