ENTRY {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "research", "d": "OK", "date": "2026-08-27", "ev": "direct reading of DEVINIT(wdc) and machine_landisk.c", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "note": "A STRUCTURAL OBSERVATION, AND THE PART OF IT I NEARLY OVERCLAIMED.\n\n*** THE ALT-STATUS ADDRESS IS COMPUTED FROM AN UNSCALED BYTE OFFSET, WHILE THE MAIN REGISTER\nBLOCK IS SCALED BY addr_mult. ***  All four rules -- the default +0x206, MACPPC's +0x160,\nHPCMIPS' hardcode and IQ80321's +0x402 -- consult `devinit->addr` and never\n`devinit->addr_mult`, while the main registration uses `DEV_WDC_LENGTH * devinit->addr_mult`.\n\nAND LANDISK IS THE ONLY IN-TREE wdc WITH addr_mult != 1 (it passes addr_mult=2; every other\ninstantiation omits it and gets 1).  So it is the only machine where the two conventions can\ndisagree -- which looked like a complete explanation for why it is the only machine whose\ndevice-control writes land nowhere.\n\n*** IT IS NOT THE EXPLANATION, AND I CHECKED BEFORE WRITING IT DOWN AS ONE. ***  Scaling does\nnot produce the measured address: 0x206 * 2 = 0x40c, and the driver writes +0x2c.  Neither\nthe raw ISA offset nor any scaling of it predicts 0x2c, so landisk's aux block genuinely\nsits somewhere machine-specific.  A `case MACHINE_LANDISK:` is therefore the RIGHT SHAPE,\nmatching MACPPC and IQ80321 -- not a paper-over of a scaling bug.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES IS A LATENT HAZARD WORTH RECORDING AND NOT FIXING: the offsets are unscaled,\nso a FUTURE machine with addr_mult != 1 that falls through to the default rule would get a\nwrong alt-status address for exactly the reason landisk does not.  Landisk escapes it only\nby getting an explicit case.  Filed as an observation on this row rather than as its own\nitem, because no such machine exists today and inventing a guard for one would be the\
ENTRY {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "research", "d": "OK", "date": "2026-08-27", "ev": "direct reading of DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus) and machine_landisk.c", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "note": "TWO OF THE PANEL'S OPEN QUESTIONS SETTLED BY READING, while the third is measured.\n\nQ3 -- WHAT DOES A MAPPED WRITE ACTUALLY DO?  The entire write arm, comments stripped, is:\n\n        if (idata & WDCTL_RST) {\n                d->cur_command = COMMAND_RESET;\n                wdc_new_command_reset(d);\n        }\n\nNothing else.  Two effects -- latch the ATAPI signature, and reset the ring/tenant state --\nand BOTH are gated on 0x04.  A read returns status_byte() and has no side effect.  So the\n'untraced code path' the steelman worries about is four lines long.\n\nP5 -- COLLISION?  NO.  landisk registers exactly one wdc block, 0x14000000-0x1400000f, and\n+0x2c lies outside it.  Nothing else in the machine claims that address.\n\n*** AND THAT SHARPENS THE ONE QUESTION THAT IS STILL OPEN INTO SOMETHING WORTH MEASURING:\nIF ANY OF THE TWELVE WRITES CARRIES WDCTL_RST (0x04), THEN MAPPING THE REGISTER STARTS\nPERFORMING REAL RESETS ON A MACHINE THAT HAS NEVER HAD ONE. ***  The prior report said six\nof twelve are 0x08 and did not record the other six.  0x08 is inert post-#481; 0x04 is not.\n\nPANEL DISPOSITION, adopted: SHIP, BUT NOT ON THIS PACKET.  The twelve-write measurement\ncomes from a LOST LOG -- the third artifact lost from the same seat's scratch directory --\nso every number in the round's warrant is a seat's report rather than a citation.  A re-take\nis in flight, and it carries the control that discriminates the two diagnoses: if the\ncommand-block accesses at +0x00..0x0f SUCCEED, the defect is a missing offset case; if they\ndo NOT, the defect is a wrong devinit->addr and the one-l
ENTRY {"seat": "opus5", "phase": "research", "d": "OK", "date": "2026-08-27", "ev": "_scratchpad/landisk_census/RESULTS.txt (+ 9 logs and every script)", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "note": "VERDICT: CONFIRMED -- the lost seat's numbers were right, AND THE RE-TAKE FOUND WHAT THE\nORIGINAL NEVER RECORDED.  The panel's gate is discharged: the warrant no longer rests on\nan unpreserved artifact.\n\nQ1 -- twelve writes to 0x1400002c, six of value 0x08?  **YES TO BOTH.**  Reproduced on\nfour runs (three pristine boots plus the instrumented build): 12 writes, 0 reads, all\nlen=1, all from pc=0x8c15f6e8 <obio_iomem_write_1+0x8>, all real guest stores\n(no_exceptions=0).\n\n*** Q2 -- THE VALUES, WHICH NO PREVIOUS REPORT CAPTURED, AND ONE OF THEM CARRIES\nWDCTL_RST. ***\n\n     1   0x0c   WDCTL_4BIT | WDCTL_RST      <-- SRST ASSERTED\n     2   0x08   WDCTL_4BIT                  <-- SRST released\n  3..12   0x02 / 0x08 alternating (IDS / 4BIT, five pairs)\n  histogram: 0x0c x1, 0x08 x6, 0x02 x5\n\nSo the FIRST write is a real ATA soft-reset assert/release pair at the head of the channel\nprobe, silently discarded today.  Bit names taken from the tree's own\nsrc/include/thirdparty/wdcreg.h, not from memory.  Confirmed independently ON THE\nUNMODIFIED BINARY by the tree's committed write-watchpoint (#250): same paddr, 0x0c first,\nsame value set.  It saw 9 of 12 because the watchpoint self-suppresses while\nabout_to_enter_single_step is set -- **a lower bound BY CONSTRUCTION**, so it corroborates\nthe VALUES and not the count, and the seat said so rather than reporting 9 as a discrepancy.\n\n  CONSEQUENCE FOR THE FIX, and it is the question this round has been holding for: mapping\n  the register makes that first write REACH `wdc_new_command_reset()` -- clearing\n  atapi_cmd_in_progress, atapi
ARTIFACTS 

ROW sgimtewidth SEV med STATE open
WITNESS harness:READ and ENUMERATED against the shipped source, 2026-08-30.  The handler is `if (len == 4) ... else if (len != 4) ... else { fatal(); exit(1); }` -- the final else is UNREACHABLE.  Bounds enumerated separately: `relative_addr &= ~CRIME_DE_START` (0x800) caps regnr at 511 against `mte_reg[1024]`, so there is NO out-of-bounds access.
NOTE *** A ONE-BYTE GUEST STORE TO AN MTE REGISTER WRITES THE REGISTER **AND SILENTLY ZEROES ITS NEIGHBOUR**.  Found by the fable5 seat under the standing license, verified here by reading the arms. ***  The handler is `if (len == 4) {...} else if (len != 4) {...} else {...}`.  The second arm does:      d->mte_reg[regnr+1] = idata >> 32;      d->mte_reg[regnr]   = idata;  and it is reached by EVERY width that is not 4 -- including 1 and 2.  For a one-byte store `idata` is a byte, so `idata >> 32` is **0**, and the store clobbers `mte_reg[regnr+1]` with zero.  No diagnostic, no decline: the device quietly loses a register the guest never addressed.  **THIS IS A MIS-SERVICE, NOT A KILL**, which is why it is med and not high: nothing ends the host, and the corruption is confined to emulated device state.  *** THE TRAP, AND IT IS THE REASON TO FILE THIS NOW RATHER THAN WHEN SOMEONE TOUCHES IT: the dead third arm is `fatal("[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]"); exit(1);` -- unreachable for every integer len, as `sgirekills` records.  It DOCUMENTS AN INTENT to reject unusual widths.  A naive future repair of the unreachable-arm defect -- reordering to `len==4 / len==8 / else` -- would RESURRECT THAT exit(1) AS A LIVE WIDTH KILL. ***  So when the unreachable arm is worked it must land as a DECLINE, never as a revived exit, and this row exists to say so before the edit rather than after.  *Two defects in one arm structure, pointing opposite ways: today it serves widths it should refuse, and the obvious tidy-up would make it kill on widths it currently serves.*  *** THE IMPLEMENTING SEAT RE-DISCOVERED THIS ARM INDEPENDENTLY ON 2026-08-30 AND PROPOSED IT AS A NEW ROW (`sgimtedeadwidth`).  FOLDED HERE, NOT FILED -- it is this row, found from the other direction. ***  It arrived via the SIBLING-PARITY check on a different round, where this row came from a pass-1 reading; that two routes reach the same arm is corroboration, not two defects.  **WHAT THE RE-DISCOVERY ADDS, and it is worth folding in:** the precise extent (`dev_sgi_re.c:1162-1178`, the unreachable `exit(1)` at `:1177`), and a COUNT this row did not carry -- **the file has EIGHT width-guard sites by text but only SEVEN LIVE ones**, because this arm's `else` can never be entered.  Verified here by opening the file: `if (len == 4) {...} else if (len != 4) {...} else { fatal(); exit(1); }`, and the second condition is the exact negation of the first.  *A duplicate row would have split one defect's evidence across two ids and let a future round close one while the other stayed open -- which is the failure the queue-id rule already names.  Adjudicated FOLD.*  *** CONFIRMED, AND THE MECHANISM IS BETTER THAN THE ROW SAID: THE WIDTH GUARD IS DEAD CODE. ***  Reaching the final `else` requires `len != 4` and `len == 4` at once, so it can never execute.  Every non-4 width falls into the 64-bit path, which does `mte_reg[regnr+1] = idata >> 32; mte_reg[regnr] = idata;` -- and a one-byte store has `idata < 256`, so the adjacent register is silently ZEROED.  **So it is not that the handler "serves every width"; it is that the guard written to refuse them cannot run.**  *** NOT A MEMORY-SAFETY DEFECT, and this was checked SPECIFICALLY because the same question was answered WRONG on `sgideoob` two days ago: *** the `&= ~CRIME_DE_START` mask caps `regnr` at 511 and `mte_reg` is `[DEV_SGI_MTE_LENGTH / 4]` = `[1024]`, so `regnr+1` reaches 512 with 512 slots of headroom.  Enumerated, not argued.  **CONSEQUENCE FOR RANKING, so no round over-claims: fixing this removes a data-corruption defect and a piece of dead code.  It does NOT remove a host kill, because that kill was never reachable.**  A round closing it and reporting "one fewer host kill" would be writing a wrong record.  *Its sibling `sgi_de` is shaped `len == 8 / len == 4 / else`, so THAT else IS reachable and `sgidewidth` is a real kill.  The two handlers differ in exactly the way that matters.*
ENTRY {"date": "2026-09-07", "phase": "assess", "seat": "codex", "note": "INDEPENDENT RE-DERIVATION during a whole-function audit of `do_mte_transfer', on a brief that did not\nmention this handler: *\"The adjacent MTE access handler also has an unreachable width-rejection branch,\nbecause `len == 4' and `len != 4' exhaust the cases.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED before recording:** `if (len == 4) {...} else if (len != 4) {...} else { fatal(...); exit(1); }'\n-- the first two arms are complementary and exhaustive, so the `else' can never run.  **The unreachable\nbranch is a HOST KILL**, which is why its deadness is load-bearing rather than cosmetic: a 1- or 2-byte\naccess falls into `len != 4' and is treated as a 64-bit access, writing `mte_reg[regnr+1]' as well as\n`mte_reg[regnr]'.\n\n**NO NEW ROW FILED.**  This row already covers it and its TITLE already anticipates the trap -- *\"the obvious\nfix for its dead arm would CREATE a width kill\"*.  Recorded as an entry because independent convergence is\nthe one kind of agreement that IS evidence, and because a second derivation of an open row is worth knowing\nwhen the round is eventually scheduled.\n\n*Second duplicate the C0 check caught in one session; the first was [[sgitexindex]].*"}

ROW wdcbeoddtail SEV med STATE open
WITNESS none
NOTE DEFECT, MEDIUM (re-rank HIGH if a real driver is measured popping the odd tail: the tell is the `[ wdc: WARNING! someone is reading too much from the wdc inbuf! ]' line in a `evbmips -e maltabe' boot log with an ATAPI image).  Site: dev_wdc.c `case wd_data:' BE assembly `odata = (odata << 8) + wdc_get_inbuf(d)' with `wdc_get_inbuf' returning (uint64_t) -1 on an empty ring; memory_writemax64 (BE) hands buf[0] bits 15:8.  Enumerated by Fable: 2-byte/1 valid -> [v-1, 0xff]; v = 0x00 -> [0xff, 0xff]; 4-byte with valid 0x12 0x00 -> [0x11, 0xff, 0xfe, 0xff].  Reachable: MALTA BE (machine_evbmips.c `MALTA (evbmips, big endian)') gets the wdc via bus_pci.c `PCIINIT(piix4_ide)'; odd lengths from `case SCSICMD_INQUIRY:' (retlen = cmd[4], min 36); the model reports the ODD byte count unpadded in wd_cyl_lo/hi, so whether a driver pops the tail is arch-specific (OpenBSD 2.2 isa/wdc.c uses bus_space_read_raw_multi_2 with the odd len).  WITNESS OWED AT RUNG 3 before any edit: `evbmips -e maltabe' + ATAPI image, INQUIRY-37 CDB via six 16-bit data-port stores then nineteen 16-bit loads -- pre-fix the 19th returns [v-1, 0xff], post-fix intact; LE control on `-e malta'; the over-read latch line is the device signature.  OPTION SET: (1) mask each pop `& 0xff' at the assembly (no other consumer changes); (2) return 0 on empty -- changes the LE pad 0xff -> 0x00 AND reds the shipped `overread -1' row (oracle-correction ORDER rule applies); (3) pad odd DATA-IN transfers to an even count and report it (BELIEVED: the ATAPI word-transfer rule; no standard on disk).  Detector: the differential's `row_odd_width' checks counters only and its memory_writemax64 stub always serialises LE -- both must change for an offline row; discharges part of wdcletwins and wdcprobeendian.  Provenance: retrospective re-check (owner directive 2026-09-04) batch 3a -- GPT-6 Astra (codex, max effort) read-only review, adjudicated by Fable 5.1; artifacts regress/artifacts/retro_20260905/batch3a_wdc/ (astra_batch3a.txt, fable_adj_batch3a.md).  The other seven panel seats were DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the retrospective (owner scope); a fix round runs the full nine-seat panel.

ROW wdcpktstatus SEV med STATE open
WITNESS none
NOTE DEFECT, MEDIUM.  Read by Fable: `wdc_command' sets `d->error = 0'; the PACKET dispatch calls diskimage_scsicommand once; on res == 0 -> PHASE_COMPLETED + int_assert, on res == 1 it tests only data_in != NULL; no `atapi_st->status' read anywhere in dev_wdc.c; `status_byte' ORs WDCS_ERR only from d->error.  Backend (diskimage_scsicmd.c): return 0 only for structural errors; return 1 for everything else INCLUDING CHECK CONDITION (`SCSI READ: refusing oversized transfer', status[0] = 0x02) and the out-of-range READ (`diskimage__return_check_condition' after memset 0 -- the #416 repair whose own comment says it alone reports a successful read of zeros); an unknown opcode returns 1 with no data -> PHASE_COMPLETED, no ERR, no message.  Guest expectation CONFIRMED on disk: _guest-src-openbsd22 sys/dev/isa/wdc.c `case PHASE_COMPLETED:' reads wd_error only `if (st & WDCS_ERR)'; atapi/atapiconf.c `atapi_sense(acp, error >> 4, flags)' decodes the sense key from bits 7:4 -- with ERR clear the guest treats the command as SUCCESS.  WITNESS OWED AT RUNG 3: landisk wdc rig (wdc_flood_probe.py machinery) or maltabe, READ(10) beyond the image end or opcode 0xff; read wd_status/wd_error: pre-fix 0x50/0x58 and 0x00, post-fix ERR set + non-zero error register; controls: a valid INQUIRY shows DRQ with ERR clear; an out-of-range CHS READ on a plain disk shows ABRT.  OPTION SET: (1) d->error = WDCE_ABRT on res == 0 and on status[0] == 0x02; (2) mirror the backend's sense key into bits 7:4 ((key << 4) | WDCE_ABRT, ILLEGAL REQUEST -> 0x54) via a diskimage sense_key accessor -- the option under which the guest's NOT_READY/UNIT_ATTENTION/RETRY logic works; (3) on CHECK CONDITION with a zeroed data_in, complete with ERR and NO DATA-IN phase.  Register-layout conformance to the standard is BELIEVED (no ATA document on disk); the in-tree guest's expectation is CONFIRMED.  Provenance: retrospective re-check (owner directive 2026-09-04) batch 3a -- GPT-6 Astra (codex, max effort) read-only review, adjudicated by Fable 5.1; artifacts regress/artifacts/retro_20260905/batch3a_wdc/ (astra_batch3a.txt, fable_adj_batch3a.md).  The other seven panel seats were DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the retrospective (owner scope); a fix round runs the full nine-seat panel.
ENTRY {"date": "2026-09-06", "phase": "review", "seat": "kimi", "note": "83df95c PASS 2.  THE ONLY SEAT THAT SETTLED THE CENSUS FROM THE CODE.  It read the data-register arm and derived the pop count as `1 + (len >= 2 ? 1 : 0) + (len == 4 ? 2 : 0)`, confirming widths 1..8 pop 1,2,2,4,2,2,2,2 -- and checked it holds under BOTH endianness branches (identical pop count, only assembly order differs), which nobody asked for.  Then answered the detector question by reading all four touched regress files: no row's oracle depends on the OLD wording, so no detector change is owed.  *** AND IT CORRECTED MY FRAMING AGAINST ITSELF: the old wording 'a 3- or 8-byte access consumes 1 or 2' is VAGUE, NOT FALSE. ***  The true value is 2 and 2 is a member of {1, 2}, so the shipped comment's trailing `\"1 or 2\" until 2026-09-05` reads as a correction of a falsehood when it is really a SHARPENING of an imprecise sentence.  Recorded as a records nuance; the correction improves the comment either way.  No verdict line (format miss).  Panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260906_211839"}
