ROW m88kdslotshadow SEV med STATE open
WITNESS none
NOTE DEFECT by reading (Fable review seat, codex, grok on the #502 pass 2; kimi DISSENTS -- see below).  Pre-existing upstream; made reachable by ORDINARY compiled code by #502 (before it, every trap-like fault in a delay slot stopped the machine at the fallback; only a trap >= 128 in a slot reached the else-arm).  Manual: pointer definitions mc88100.txt:2097-2104 (FIP = XIP + d26 while the branch is in XIP; one stage later, while the SLOT executes, NIP = target, FIP = target+4), :4207-4208, :8898-8902, Table 6-3; X(rte) (anchor `The NIP instruction should first be executed') executes the SNIP instruction then jumps to SFIP when SFIP != SNIP+4.  The fork's own DATA_ACCESS delay-slot arm two lines up already has the manual's shape (SNIP = delay_target | V; SFIP = SNIP + 4) -- the in-tree precedent, exercised by OpenBSD on every page fault in a delay slot.  #502's detector row `dslot' PINS THE ARM AS IMPLEMENTED (labelled so in the probe: br.n +16 -> SNIP 0x1000e, SFIP 0x10016); when this round lands the row is RE-PINNED FIRST (oracle-correction rule: re-pin, measure HEAD red, then fix).  Rows for this round: dslot16 (fork today SNIP 0x1000e/SFIP 0x10016; manual 0x10016/0x1001a; a no-special-case arm 0x1000e/0x10012 -- three distinct pairs, one row discriminates all three) and dslotnt (a NOT-taken bcnd.n with a faulting slot: fork SFIP == SNIP; manual SFIP = SNIP+4), plus a handler-RETURN row (rte + counters at fallthrough/target/target+4 -- codex) that observes the execution sequence, not only the registers.  KIMI'S DISSENT, verbatim: The manual's SNIP/SFIP pipeline-shadow semantics at 6.4.3.1.2/.3 do support the code's else-arm: SNIP = shadow of NIP, SFIP = "the target of a jump or branch instruction."  The pre-existing DATA_ACCESS-in-delay-slot arm, SNIP = delay_target, is the odd one out against that reading.  The two readings predict DIFFERENT post-rte execution sequences, so the handler-return row settles it by measurement.  Severity MEDIUM by reasoning (no OpenBSD luna88k source on disk to show a guest path that depends on it).  Provenance: #502 pass 2 (fable_p2.md finding 1; codex pass-2 answer; grok verdict) and the #502 adjudication pass.

