ENTRY {"seat": "fable5", "phase": "design", "d": "2026-09-09", "date": "2026-09-09", "note": "ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  RULING on the three design questions.  (1) THE ACCEPT ROWS ARE OWED BEFORE SHIPPING, and there are THREE, not two.  This is the de-escalation clause exactly: the fix ADDS a predicate and every bound-moving mutant (`>= 2', `<= 2', `<= 4') escapes because no driven value sits at a boundary.  The anchor rule's accept-side lesson applies: a row at ONE boundary pins only that boundary.  So: a0 == 1 takes VADDR (kills `>= 2'); a0 == 3 takes VADDR (kills `<= 2'); AND a0 == 4 takes ALL (kills `<= 4') -- the third cell the question's own mutant list names and its row list omits.  'An unrelated cached translation SURVIVES' is the right oracle, because survival is what the wrong arm produces; each row must be shown to REDDEN under its bound-moving mutant (accept-row failability), not merely to exist.  (2) YES: cite the manual for the ITBASM MECHANISM only; the selectivity conclusion rests on the cache entry's structure (no ASM/ASN metadata), verified.  (3) SILENCE, with a comment citing why: over-invalidation cannot serve stale data (glm is right), the addition licence forbids an announce with no measured delta, and the house latched-announce idiom is for guest-drivable DEFECTS, not for conservative-correct paths.  A debug-level line would be a check that cannot fail in the state that matters.  ALSO OWED, from #514 landing first (glm's warning, ratified): this witness must be RE-BASELINED on a tree carrying #514 -- checkable: compare the witness
