2: 
3: Please do not simply ratify. Check the premises, not just the derivations. Prose only -- no patch to apply,
4: though you may SKETCH an edit against the full function inlined below, which is why it is inlined. VERDICT
5: LINE FIRST. Ordinary bounds-correctness engineering on a bus-bridge byte-lane handler.
6: 
7: ## THE TREE
8: `C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\`. Ignore any other `src/` under the project root. Read
9: `src/devices/dev_lca.c` (working tree; the `#518` diff is uncommitted and touches only `lca_ioc`), and your
10: own earlier assessment at `regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/codex_assess_20260908.txt` (it censused the idiom
11: across five files, bounded the severities, found the obvious one-character fix makes things WORSE, and noted
12: a double-count with lcabadval -- that exit is now ASSIGNED to this row).
13: 
14: ## SETTLED
15: S1. THE ROW: `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa)` -- an off-by-one guard (`ofs > len`, where `ofs == len` is accepted)
16:     that lets a bus-hitting read return all zeros; the downstream `memory_rw` result discarded; and an
17:     uninitialised byte consumed on that failure. The `ofs > len` branch is a `fatal()` + `exit(1)` -- the last
18:     executable process exit in this file after `#516` and `#518`.
19: S2. The 2026-09-09 adjudication assigned the `ofs > len` exit to THIS row (it had been double-counted with
20:     lcabadval). The option set for an exit is: 1 delete, 2 silent decline, 3 decline + latched announce,
