FILE regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/codex_assess_20260908.txt
1: **Verdict: both a guest-visible nondeterminism defect and a bounded information-exposure defect.** The source establishes a path from uninitialised emulator storage to guest memory; it does not establish sensitive contents or measurable severity. Two qualifications matter: RGB GET reads only three bytes, and zero-initialisation does not remove the `random()` behaviour.
2: 
3: **Q1. PUT:** Each cell below is **bytes written by the switch / uninitialised bytes transferred**. Counts follow the current [PUT switch](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:307).
4: 
5: | Colour type | Depth 1 | Depth 2 | Depth 4 | Depth 8 |
6: |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
7: | CI | 1 / 0 | 1 / **1** | 1 / **3** | 1 / **7** |
8: | RGB | 1 / 0 | 2 / 0 | 4 / 0 | 4 / **4** |
9: | RGBA | 1 / 0 | 2 / 0 | 4 / 0 | 4 / **4** |
10: | ABGR | 1 / 0 | 2 / 0 | 4 / 0 | 4 / **4** |
11: | YCRCB, through `default` | 1 / 0 | 2 / 0 | 4 / 0 | 4 / **4** |
12: | Each other encoding, `0x40` through `0xe0` | 1 / 0 | 2 / 0 | 4 / 0 | 4 / **4** |
13: 
14: `random()` **initialises** its four destination bytes, although their values are objectionable for a separate reason.
15: 
16: There are:
17: 
18: - **6 affected combinations out of 16** for the four explicitly handled types.
19: - **7 out of 20** including the fifth named type, YCRCB.
20: - **18 out of 64** across all sixteen encodings allowed by the [type mask](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h:328): three CI combinations plus depth 8 for each other encoding.
21: 
22: These are colour-type × byte-depth counts. They assume the call passes the coordinate/TLB/pointer guards and reaches writable guest RAM. No colour/depth pairing validation prevents those combinations. The default arm’s [`fatal()` only logs](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:384), so it does not prevent the transfer. The RAM path [copies the requested bytes](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:778).
23: 
24: **Q2. GET:** Assuming the read supplies all `bufdepth` bytes, the following counts are **uninitialised bytes consumed by recombination**:
25: 
26: | Colour type | Bytes recombined | Depth 1 | Depth 2 | Depth 4 | Depth 8 |
27: |---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
28: | CI | `buf[0]` | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
29: | RGB | `buf[0..2]` | **2** | **1** | 0 | 0 |
30: | RGBA | `buf[0..3]` | **3** | **2** | 0 | 0 |
31: | ABGR | `buf[0..3]` | **3** | **2** | 0 | 0 |
32: | YCRCB, through `default` | `buf[0..3]` | **3** | **2** | 0 | 0 |
33: | Each other encoding | `buf[0..3]` | **3** | **2** | 0 | 0 |
34: 
35: Thus **6/16**, **8/20**, or **30/64**, using the same populations as Q1. The current [RGB recombination](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:373) never reads `buf[3]`.
36: 
37: The unwritten elements are **uninitialised on every invocation**, including the first. Reused stack storage can physically contain bytes from an earlier call—or unrelated earlier work—but there is no persistent previous-call buffer in the language semantics. Automatic storage becomes indeterminate again on each execution of the declaration. [C11 draft, §6.2.4 and §6.7.9](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf)
38: 
39: Those bytes participate directly in the assignment to `*color`, reaching the caller’s object. In [rectangle drawing](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1007), the source GET updates `color`, which can subsequently reach the destination PUT. Other GETs update `oldcolor` for raster operations. **Not every affected GET necessarily exposes every affected byte:** subsequent processing, truncation or suppression can discard them.
40: 
41: The current tiled MTE constructs CI/1, CI/2, RGBA/4 and CI/8. Its successful GETs therefore avoid this short-read recombination problem; its CI/2 and CI/8 PUTs remain affected. See the [MTE mode construction and calls](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1498).
42: 
43: The GET table assumes a fully supplied read. The helper ignores the memory operation’s return value; a read leaving additional bytes untouched can broaden the problem.
44: 
45: **Q3. Exposure:** These are bytes from the emulator process’s own automatic storage. Guest output can consequently depend on host execution history, and previously stored process data can cross into guest-visible memory. That supports **both classifications**.
46: 
47: The bound here is up to **seven unwritten bytes per PUT**, or **three bytes incorporated into a GET result**. This defect does not require reading beyond `buf[8]`. The contents might be zeros, guest-derived data, or other process data. Source inspection alone does not demonstrate secrets, another process’s memory, or a reliable arbitrary host-memory read.
48: 

FILE regress/alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: #
3: #  alphapfnoffset -- RUNG-3 WITNESS: the Alpha page-table walk drops the within-page offset.
4: #
5: #      memory_alpha.c   (*return_paddr) = ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift;
6: #      memory_rw.c      offset = paddr & offset_mask;
7: #
8: #  The walk returns a PAGE BASE, so `paddr & offset_mask' is zero and a walked access resolves to
9: #  the page's first bytes.  A codex reachability pass enumerated all 8192 offsets: the walk yields
10: #  offset zero throughout, differing from the offset-preserving address for 8191 of them.
11: #
12: #  *** THIS IS A WITNESS (rung 3), NOT A DETECTOR.  It shows the symptom on the COMMITTED build,
13: #  which is what licenses a fix, and it must NOT be gated -- a witness stays red until the fix
14: #  lands.  Its rows therefore assert the CORRECT behaviour and are expected to FAIL here. ***
15: #
16: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
17: #  SIX THINGS THE DESIGN REVIEW (grok, 2026-09-07) ESTABLISHED.  Four are traps that would have
18: #  produced a GREEN run on the defective build; none is obvious from the source.
19: #
20: #  1. THE SWAP IS NOT A BLOCKER.  `swpctx' is the only writer of apcb_ptbr -- but it is only a
21: #     CONFOUND when ptbr and flags DIFFER.  Put the SAME L1 PFN in both PCB fields and the
22: #     defective line and the corrected line both leave apcb_ptbr = L1_PFN.  (The inverse is a
23: #     trap: PFN in flags with ptbr = 0 arms the walk on HEAD and leaves IDENTITY MAPPING on a
24: #     fixed PAL, so post-fix green would be the bootstrap, not the repair.)
25: #
26: #  2. PFN, NOT BYTE ADDRESS.  apcb_ptbr and the PTE high halves hold PFNs.  ptbr = 0x01050000
27: #     would give base = 0x01050000 << 13, past RAM, and the walk reports `wasn't found'.
28: #
29: #  3. ONE COLD SHOT.  The fast path caches the page from `paddr & ~offset_mask' -- the CORRECT
30: #     physical page -- so the walked paddr is wrong only for the FILL.  A second access at any
31: #     offset is a hit and reads the right bytes, i.e. GOES GREEN ON HEAD.  Load once; never retry.
32: #
33: #  4. THE POLARITY TRAP.  Planting B *through the walk* makes HEAD store it at data+0 and read it
34: #     back from data+0 -- the defect measured against itself, green.  A cousin: planting at vaddr
35: #     0/N while ptbr == 0 hits PHYSICAL 0/N, not the data page.  So A and B are planted through
36: #     K0SEG of the DATA PAGE, before the walk is ever used.
37: #
38: #  5. A FAILED WALK DOES NOT WRITE THE DESTINATION REGISTER.  LS_GENERIC_N returns on a memory_rw
39: #     failure without storing, so "faults or returns zero" is false.  Hence a THREE-WAY oracle
40: #     with a sentinel S pre-loaded into the destination:
41: #         S = the walk never completed        0 = walked onto unmapped physical
42: #         A = right page, offset dropped      B = correct
43: #
44: #  6. ONLY ALPHA_PTE_VALID IS LOAD-BEARING.  Verified in memory_alpha.c: each level tests
45: #     `!(pteN & ALPHA_PTE_VALID)' and nothing else -- not KR/KW/UR/UW, FAULT_ON_*, ASM or
46: #     granularity.  Setting bits the walk does not implement and calling them required would be
47: #     the "cannot hurt" addition this project refuses.
48: #

FILE regress/sgi_mte_fill_probe.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: #
3: #  sgimtedepth4dead -- RUNG-3 WITNESS: `if (depth == 4)' is dead, so MTE never selects RGBA.
4: #
5: #      :1311  int depth = 8 << ((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT);   /* BITS */
6: #      :1359  src_x1 /= (depth / 8);                     <- the author converting bits -> bytes
7: #      :1369  // ... this makes sure that we copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit transfers.
8: #      :1371  if (depth == 4) { src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA; dst_mode |= ...; }
9: #
10: #  The field is two bits, so `depth' is 8/16/32/64 -- never 4.  The branch is dead, the constructed
11: #  modes carry no type bits, `color_mode' stays CI, and CI's PUT assigns buf[0] only while the
12: #  transfer moves `bufdepth' bytes.
13: #
14: #  *** IT BEGAN AS A 6-ROW WITNESS, BECAME A 14-ROW DETECTOR, AND IS 16 ROWS AS OF 2026-09-09.
15: #  The banner that used to stand here -- "a witness, not a detector; its rows are expected to
16: #  FAIL; it must not be gated" -- was true of the 6-row version against HEAD and is FALSE of this
17: #  one.  The pass-2 codex seat caught that staleness; the ROW COUNT in the replacement then went
18: #  stale in its turn, on the very next round to touch the file.
19: #
20: #  *** SO NO SCORE IS TRANSCRIBED HERE ANY MORE.  Every table this instrument has produced sits
21: #  under regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/ -- killtable_20260907 (13 rows), killtable2_20260907
22: #  (14), killtable3_20260909 (14, the label-blindness measurement), repair_20260909 (16, the
23: #  table that shipped the two label rows) -- and each log carries its own MTEFILL_RESULT line and
24: #  a MTEFILL_DONE sentinel.  Read the log.  A count in a header decays the moment a row is added,
25: #  and this one already did, twice. ***
26: #
27: #  It is STILL NOT WIRED INTO A GATE, and that is a separate decision from what it is.
28: #
29: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
30: #  THE ORACLE IS `dest[0]' OF A TILED **FILL**, AND EVERY OTHER CHANNEL I PROPOSED WAS REFUSED.
31: #  From the grok design review, each point measured against the source rather than argued:
32: #
33: #  * A PRE-PAINTED TAIL CANNOT WORK.  CI PUT does not replicate buf[0] across the span and does not
34: #    leave a tail: it writes buf[0] at the lowest address and buf[1..bufdepth-1] as uninitialised
35: #    stack, and memory_rw memcpys `bufdepth' bytes.  So the paint is OVERWRITTEN WITH GARBAGE.  A row
36: #    asserting "tail still painted" reds on HEAD because the path RAN -- which reads as "the witness
37: #    never reached MTE".  A row asserting "tail != paint" is true on HEAD *and* after the fix.
38: #
39: #  * COPY CANNOT WITNESS THIS AT ALL.  Source `aa bb cc dd': CI GET takes buf[0] == 0xaa and CI PUT
40: #    writes 0xaa at dest[0]; RGBA GET reconstructs 0xaabbccdd and RGBA PUT also writes 0xaa there.
41: #    The first byte of a copy is the one byte CI already gets right, and the broken bytes are exactly
42: #    the uninitialised ones that must not be read.  "If the witness is a blit, refuse it."
43: #
44: #  * FILL IS DETERMINISTIC BECAUSE IT NEVER GETs.  With bg = 0x11223344:
45: #        CI   PUT -> buf[0] = (uint8_t)bg          -> dest[0] = 0x44   (the LOW byte)
46: #        RGBA PUT -> [bg>>24, bg>>16, bg>>8, bg]   -> dest[0] = 0x11   (the HIGH byte)
47: #    dest[1..3] are uninitialised on HEAD.  **DO NOT ASSERT THEM THERE.**
48: #

FILE regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: #
3: #  sgirergbroundtrip -- RUNG-3 WITNESS for dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel()'s RGB arms.
4: #
5: #  COPIED from regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py, whose session already drives an XFER copy
6: #  through this helper on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32'.  Only the SOURCE COLOUR TYPE changes.
7: #
8: #  THE DEFECT: the RGB PUT and GET arms disagree about which byte holds which channel.
9: #      PUT  buf[0] = c>>24;  buf[1] = c>>16;  buf[2] = c>>8;  buf[3] = 0;
10: #      GET  *color = (buf[1]<<24) + (buf[2]<<16) + (buf[3]<<8);
11: #  PUT writes the top three bytes at 0,1,2; GET reads 1,2,3.  RGB is the ONLY multi-byte mode
12: #  that fails its own round trip -- RGBA and ABGR both round-trip, using OPPOSITE byte orders,
13: #  which is why this is an internal inconsistency and not an endianness question.
14: #
15: #  WHY THE ORACLE IS A VALUE AND NOT AN INEQUALITY: an XFER copy GETs with src_mode and PUTs
16: #  with dst_mode, which are INDEPENDENT guest registers.  With src RGB and dst RGBA the
17: #  corruption is a computable one-byte left shift with a zero fill, so the row asserts an exact
18: #  value on both the defective and the fixed build.  No uninitialised byte is read, so the
19: #  `separate automatic objects have no architectural promise' objection that killed a
20: #  neighbouring witness design does not apply here -- checked, not assumed.
21: #
22: #  (original header follows)
23: #  sgirandompixel -- the probe this was copied from.
24: #
25: #  THE DEFECT.  horrible_getputpixel() extracts `tlb_mode = (mode >> 10) & 0x7' and switches on it.
26: #  Cases 0, 1, 2 and 4 are implemented.  The `default:' arm prints, has its exit(1) COMMENTED OUT,
27: #  and then executes `*color = random(); return;'.  So a failed GET does not decline -- it hands the
28: #  caller a random 32-bit value.  In draw_primitive() the failed source GET and the destination PUT
29: #  share one `color' variable in one loop iteration:
30: #
31: #      if (drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN)
32: #              horrible_getputpixel(0, ..., src_x, src_y, &color, src_mode);   // may set random()
33: #      ...
34: #      if (draw)
35: #              horrible_getputpixel(1, ..., x, y, &color, dst_mode);           // writes it
36: #
37: #  AND THE UNHANDLED VALUE IS A DOCUMENTED MODE.  src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h defines five:
38: #    DE_MODE_TLB_A 0x0000 -> 0 handled     DE_MODE_LIN_A 0x1000 -> 4 handled
39: #    DE_MODE_TLB_B 0x0400 -> 1 handled     DE_MODE_LIN_B 0x1400 -> 5 *** default -> random() ***
40: #    DE_MODE_TLB_C 0x0800 -> 2 handled
41: #
42: #  CONTRACT (vacuity-floor clause (e)) -- what this witness catches and what it does NOT:
43: #    CATCHES   the RGB GET arm re-indexing: `rgb corrupt' pins the EXACT post-GET value, so a
44: #              destination receives anything other than a value distinct from BOTH the source and
45: #              the pre-painted destination.  Restoring `exit(1)' there kills the machine and reds
46: #              fix that merely changes the value without correcting the byte positions still reds.
47: #              ONLY if the pre-paint is removed -- which is why the destination is PRE-PAINTED with a
48: #              known value and the row rejects it: "left alone" and "wrote random" are then distinct.

FILE regress/arm_bfx_width_probe.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: #
3: #  armbfxwidth32 -- RUNG-3 WITNESS: UBFX/SBFX build their mask with a shift that is undefined
4: #  at the top of the encoded width range.
5: #
6: #      cpu_arm_instr.c:4486  ubfx:  int width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31);   -> {1..32}
7: #      cpu_arm_instr.c:4493  sbfx:  int width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31);   -> {1..32}
8: #      cpu_arm_instr.c:1316  ubfx:  uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
9: #      cpu_arm_instr.c:1340  sbfx:  uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
10: #      cpu_arm_instr.c:1344  sbfx:  uint32_t topBitMask = 1 << (width-1);
11: #      cpu_arm_instr.c:1370  bfi :  uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;       (width = msb-lsb+1)
12: #
13: #  `1' is a plain int, so `1 << 32' shifts by the full width of the type (C99 6.5.7p3) and
14: #  `1 << 31' is a signed overflow (6.5.7p4).
15: #
16: #  *** WHICH OF THOSE IS OBSERVABLE IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION, AND ONLY ONE IS. ***  Derived by
17: #  walking the values, against the pass-1 panel's own framing of "two widths wide":
18: #
19: #      width  correct       predicted today   observable?
20: #      32     0x89abcdef    0x00000000        YES -- the whole register becomes zero
21: #      31     0x09abcdef    0x09abcdef        no -- `1<<31' gives INT_MIN and INT_MIN-1 wraps
22: #                                             back to INT_MAX = 0x7fffffff, the CORRECT mask
23: #       8     0x000000de    0x000000de        no
24: #       1     0x00000001    0x00000001        no
25: #
26: #  So the RED row is width 32 alone.  The width-31 row is carried anyway, because it pins that a
27: #  repair does not BREAK the case that currently works by accident.
28: #
29: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
30: #  *** THE HAND-ASSEMBLED-ENCODING TRAP IS LOAD-BEARING HERE, NOT MERELY PRUDENT. ***
31: #
32: #  This project has been bitten four times by hand-assembled encodings, once in a COMMITTED gate
33: #  row that measured the wrong register for months.  The signature is always the same: a wrong
34: #  field yields 0, and any row that accepts 0 passes by accident.
35: #
36: #  **The width-32 symptom IS exactly zero.**  So a mis-encoded word that never executed is
37: #  indistinguishable from the defect this probe exists to witness.  THREE independent controls,
38: #  because one is not enough:
39: #
40: #    * `pc adv'  -- pc must advance by exactly 4.  A word the decoder cannot translate does not
41: #                  quietly do nothing.  *** AN EARLIER DRAFT SAID IT ALWAYS REACHES THE SHARED
42: #                  `bad' LABEL AND SETS cpu->running = 0.  THAT IS STALE: `#312' added an
43: #                  UNDEFINED-INSTRUCTION route, and cpu_arm_instr.c says so in its own words --
44: #                  "Routing to und fixes that", "This is where the und routing lives now" -- while
45: #                  a `goto bad' remains for other cases.  So the claim is true of SOME encodings
46: #                  and false as stated.  Either way pc does not land on CODE+4 with r0 set, which
47: #                  is what this row actually tests. ***
48: #    * `mid'     -- UBFX r0,r1,#4,#8 must give 0xde.  A small nonzero answer that only a correctly

FILE regress/wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py
1: """wdcaddtoinbufbound -- RUNG-3 WITNESS.  Real guest instructions, real address
2: decode, real memory_rw, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description
3: (`evbmips -e malta`).  No device is added and no machine description is edited.
4: 
5: THE DEFECT.  The guest WRITE arm of the wdc data port pushes bytes into the
6: shared inbuf ring with NO phase guard.  During an ATAPI DATA-IN transfer the
7: one diagnostic that could announce a stray write is suppressed, because its
8: condition requires !atapi_cmd_in_progress and that flag is 1 for the whole of
9: DATAIN.  Injected bytes grow `head` WITHOUT touching `atapi_len`, so they sit
10: in the ring ahead of the next chunk and the guest's whole next chunk arrives
11: SHIFTED.
12: 
13: THE ORACLE IS ABSOLUTE, NOT RELATIVE.  Chunk 2's bytes are poisoned at known
14: image offsets, so the fixed leg's expected values are tied to DISK CONTENT
15: through a channel outside the ring -- not merely "different from the shipped
16: leg".
17: 
18: THE PATH (all references GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c at HEAD c125450):
19:   wd_command(7) <- 0xa0   wdc_command() ATAPI_PKT_CMD arm: sets
20:                           atapi_cmd_in_progress=1, atapi_phase=PHASE_CMDOUT.
21:   wd_data(0)   <- 12 CDB bytes (READ(10), LBA 0, 128 blocks -- see READ_BLOCKS
22:                           below for why 128 and not 32).  On the 12th,
23:                           inbuf_len==12 and the dispatch
24:                           fires (guarded by atapi_cmd_in_progress && !badlen_this
25:                           && atapi_phase==PHASE_CMDOUT && inbuf_len==12).
26:                           diskimage_scsicommand() READ(10) -> res==1,
27:                           data_in_len=65536; atapi_len clamped to 32768 and
28:                           32768 bytes pushed; atapi_phase=PHASE_DATAIN.
29:   wd_data(0)   -> 32767 byte reads.  Each pops one byte and decrements
30:                           atapi_len (consumed = len==4?4:(len>=2?2:1), clamped
31:                           to what remains).  atapi_len -> 1, ring holds 1.
32:   wd_data(0)   <- ONE 2-byte store.  The write arm's `case 2:` pushes
33:                           idata&0xff then (idata>>8)&0xff -- 0xa5 then 0x5a for
34:                           0x5aa5 little-endian.  atapi_len is UNTOUCHED.
35:   wd_data(0)   -> one byte read.  Pops the last chunk-1 byte, atapi_len -> 0,
36:                           and the re-arm fires: atapi_received(32768) <
37:                           data_in_len(65536), so atapi_len = 32768 and
38:                           data_in[32768+j] is pushed ONTO THE DIRTY RING.
39:   wd_data(0)   -> the next reads.  *** SHIPPED: the guest is handed its own
40:                           injected bytes back as disk data.  FIXED: chunk 2. ***
41: 
42: STATUS ORACLE, PINNED FROM SOURCE AND NOT FROM A MEASUREMENT.  status_byte()
43: ORs DRDY|DSC (0x50, diskimage_exist true), DRQ (0x08) when head != tail, DRQ
44: again when atapi_cmd_in_progress && (atapi_phase & WDCS_DRQ) -- PHASE_DATAIN is
45: WDCS_DRQ|WDCI_IN so that arm is set too -- and ERR (0x01) only when d->error.
46: wdc_command() assigns `d->error = 0` at its top, so after ATAPI_PKT_CMD the ERR
47: bit is CLEAR.  Expected: 0x58.  (The wdcringambig probe sees 0x59 because it
48: issues no command at all and the power-on d->error = 1 stands.)

FILE regress/wdc_altoffset_probe.py
1: """wdcaltoffset -- RUNG-3 WITNESS AND DETECTOR.  Real guest instructions, real address
2: decode, real memory_rw, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description (evbmips -e malta).
3: 
4: THE DEFECT.  DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus) in src/devices/dev_wdc.c reads relative_addr
5: ZERO times, while dev_wdc_init registers its region TWO BYTES WIDE.  The durable keys are
6: grep anchors, not line numbers:
7: 
8:     grep -n 'DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus)'   src/devices/dev_wdc.c
9:     grep -n '"wdc_altstatus",'               src/devices/dev_wdc.c
10: 
11:         memory_device_register(..., "wdc_altstatus", alt_status_addr, 2,
12:             dev_wdc_altstatus_access, d, DM_DEFAULT, NULL);
13: 
14:     offset 0  ->  0x3f6  Device Control      (write: nIEN, SRST, 4BIT)
15:     offset 1  ->  0x3f7  Drive Address Reg   READ-ONLY on real ATA hardware
16: 
17: Both offsets land in the same handler body, so a guest STORE to 0x3f7 is executed as a
18: store to Device Control.  Since #481 corrected the arm's bit to WDCTL_RST and wired in
19: wdc_new_command_reset(), that store now performs a full soft reset: write_in_progress is
20: cleared and the input ring is purged.  A write to a register the guest is not even
21: allowed to write aborts a live ATA WRITE SECTORS and destroys its staged bytes.
22: 
23: THE MACHINE IS NOT MODIFIED AND NO DEVICE IS ADDED.  malta carries a wdc through its own
24: committed path: machine_evbmips.c bus_pci_add(..., "piix4_ide") -> bus_pci.c
25: PCIINIT(piix4_ide), which does device_add(machine, "wdc addr=<isa_portbase+0x1f0> ...")
26: whenever an IDE image is attached; isa_portbase = 0x18000000.  Attaching a disk image is
27: ordinary operation, not a machine edit.
28: 
29:     wdc registers      physical 0x180001f0 + N  ->  KSEG1 0xffffffffb80001f0 + N
30:     wdc_altstatus      physical 0x180003f6      ->  KSEG1 0xffffffffb80003f6
31:     the read-only reg  physical 0x180003f7      ->  KSEG1 0xffffffffb80003f7
32: 
33: THE SHAPE OF THE WITNESS -- FOUR phases, IDENTICAL except for the address of one
34: store (phases 2 and 4 differ only in the VALUE stored).
35: 
36:     PHASE 1  poke 0x06 (nIEN|SRST) at 0x3f6, the REAL Device Control register.
37:              A legitimate soft reset.  BOTH builds must abort the armed write.
38:              This is the ACCEPT control the de-escalation clause demands: it proves the
39:              fix leg does not disable the register it is supposed to leave alone, and it
