457: 
458: 
459: /*  pcc2widthkills: decline a wide WRITE to a byte register, announcing once per
460:     block per instance.  The caller `break's after this; the read side of the same
461:     block never reaches here.  */
462: static void pcc2_width_decline(struct pcc2_data *d, int bit, const char *name, int len)
463: {
464: 	if (!(d->warned & (1u << bit))) {
465: 		d->warned |= (1u << bit);
466: 		fatal("[ pcc2: unimplemented len=%i write to %s -- declined."
467: 		    "  (once) ]\n", len, name);
468: 	}
469: }
470: 
471: DEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2)
472: {
473: 	uint64_t idata = 0;
474: 	struct pcc2_data *d = (struct pcc2_data *) extra;
475: 
476: 	/*  0xfff42000..0xfff42fff, but only 0x40 unique registers:  */
477: 	relative_addr %= PCC2_SIZE;
478: 
479: 	if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)
480: 		idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len);
481: 
482: 	if (writeflag == MEM_READ) {
483: 		if (relative_addr + len <= PCC2_SIZE)	/* OB-11: end-span */
484: 			memcpy(data, d->pcctwo_reg + relative_addr, len);
485: 		else {
486: 			static int warned = 0;	/* #119: loud-once (course-correction) */
487: 			if (!warned) {
488: 				fatal("[ pcc2: read 0x%x+%i beyond reg space 0x%x; "
489: 				    "zero-filled ]\n", (int)relative_addr, (int)len,
490: 				    (int)PCC2_SIZE);
491: 				warned = 1;
492: 			}
493: 			memset(data, 0, len);	/* OB-11b: don't leak uninit host memory */
494: 		}
495: 	}
496: 
497: 	switch (relative_addr) {
498: 
499: 	case PCCTWO_CHIPID:
500: 	case PCCTWO_CHIPREV:
501: 		if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {
502: 			fatal("[ IGNORING write to read-only PCCTWO_CHIPID/"
503: 			    "CHIPREV register ]\n");
504: 		}
505: 		break;
506: 
507: 	case PCCTWO_GENCTL:
508: 		if (len != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {
509: 			pcc2_width_decline(d, PCC2_W_GENCTL_LEN, "PCCTWO_GENCTL", len);
510: 			break;
511: 		}

FILE regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/codex_assess_20260908.txt
1: **VERDICT: Four behavioral issues, one conditional initialization gap, and one minor format mismatch. The filed “three” overstates the initialization claim. These are several candidate rounds, with one already-recorded site requiring explicit rescoping.**
2: 
3: This review used the fork’s source and permitted regression/documentation files. I made no changes and ran no probes.
4: 
5: The grep results give useful precedents:
6: 
7: - The same function’s **write arm already returns the downstream result**. That is the strongest precedent for repairing its read arm.
8: - Both sibling handlers initialize `odata = 0`: [`lca_pci_conf`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:91) and [`lca_ioc`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:217). The configuration handler also has a nonterminating fallback, `dev = 0`, at line 163. That supports avoiding termination, but does **not** establish correct ISA lane semantics.
9: - Comparable bridge handlers [`eagle`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_eagle.c:52) and [`footbridge_isa`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_footbridge.c:411) initialize read results; Footbridge warns about unsupported writes without exiting. Footbridge’s handler acknowledges interrupts—it is not an equivalent sparse-lane mapper.
10: - The closest forwarding precedent is [`DEV_RAM_MIRROR`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_ram.c:77): it passes `PHYSICAL` and returns the nested result.
11: - For unsupported access widths, the ISA-connected [`wdc`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:1488) already declines and latches its announcement. I found no second implementation of LCA’s sparse-lane packing in the fork.
12: 
13: 1. **The lane boundary is inconsistent with the read and write operations.**  
14:    [`dev_lca.c:193`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:193):
15: 
16:    ```c
17:    if (ofs > len) {
18:    ```
19: 
20:    Equality passes. The later write expression, `byte = data[ofs % len];`, then selects byte zero; the read expression, `data[i] = i == ofs? byte : 0x00;`, never places the downstream byte anywhere. Nevertheless, the downstream read has occurred and may have side effects.
21: 
22:    Under this function’s existing “selected lane must exist in the buffer” contract, rejection must include equality: **`ofs >= len`**. Accepted accesses can then index `data[ofs]` directly.
23: 
24:    This is **not a demonstrated buffer overrun**: positive-length writes use modulo, and the read loop stays within `len`. The modulo is part of the incorrect boundary behavior, not another independent OOB finding. A zero-length write introduces a separate modulo-zero possibility, but no zero-length guest access was established.
25: 
26: 2. **The rejected-lane disposition terminates the emulator.**  
27:    [`dev_lca.c:196`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:196):
28: 
29:    ```c
30:    exit(1);
31:    ```
32: 
33:    This converts an unsupported lane/container combination into process termination. Changing only `>` to `>=` would expand that termination behavior.
34: 
35:    My interim disposition is **3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE**: omit the downstream access, define the declined read result, discard the write, and announce once per LCA instance. A zero-filled read is an explicit emulation fallback here, not a claim that hardware returned zero.
36: 
37:    This site is already described in the [`lcabadval` range-site note](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:15491). Do not file its termination as a new discovery.
38: 
39: 3. **The read arm discards downstream failure and reports success.**  
40:    [`dev_lca.c:205`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:205):
41: 
42:    ```c
43:    cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, LCA_ISA_BASE + relative_addr,
44:        &byte, 1, MEM_READ, CACHE_NONE);
45:    ```
46: 
47:    followed by:
48: 
49:    ```c
50:    return 1;
51:    ```
52: 
53:    The write arm preserves the nested status; the read arm replaces it with success and continues assembling data.
54: 
55:    It should **check the nested result before consuming `byte`, and preserve failure**. This is **7 IMPLEMENT**, meaning complete the forwarding contract. Simply initializing `byte` would leave this defect intact.
56: 
57:    On Alpha, preserving failure still matters: the [load helper](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c:68) returns before destination-register writeback when `memory_rw` fails. It does **not** thereby deliver a guest exception.
58: 
59: 4. **The uninitialized-byte claim has a narrow surviving case, not the filed general explanation.**  
60:    [`dev_lca.c:188`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:188):
61: 
62:    ```c
63:    uint8_t byte;
64:    ```
65: 
66:    The fork already zeroes read buffers on [failed translation](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:259), and [before device dispatch](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:485). Ordinary unmapped reads also receive zeros. Therefore, “a failed read leaves `byte` uninitialized” is generally false in this fork.
67: 
68:    One exception remains: the [halt-on-nonexistent-memory path](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:675) returns before zero-fill. LCA can then copy an indeterminate byte into its caller’s buffer. However, that path stops the CPUs, and the [outer device dispatcher](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:586) detects the running-to-stopped transition and returns failure. Normal Alpha load writeback is consequently suppressed.
69: 
70:    **Withdraw the broad guest-visible disclosure claim.** Retain a conditional initialization gap: initialize `byte` and avoid consuming failed results. Initializing it is **7 IMPLEMENT** for defined output handling; repairing item 3 can also eliminate this failed-read consumption without a separate initialization change.
71: 
72: 5. **Both forwarding calls treat a physical ISA address as a virtual address.**  
73:    [`dev_lca.c:202`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:202) and line 206 pass:
74: 

