FILE OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md
13705: > **A2's inertness is the correction to carry forward**: the witness's reset-value case used `x`,
13706: > which is not a debugger command, so it never touched memory in EITHER build and its own grep
13707: > filtered the error.  The detector's `reset` rows supersede it by asserting the DRAW message.
13708: 
13709: 
13710: `draw_primitive()` reads its op straight from a guest-written register:
13711: 
13712: ```c
13713: uint32_t op = d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE - 0x2000) / sizeof(uint32_t)];
13714: ...
13715: switch (op & 0xff000000) {
13716: case DE_PRIM_LINE:       ...
13717: case DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE:  ...
13718: default: fatal("[ sgi_de: UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op = 0x%08x, ... ]"); exit(1);
13719: }
13720: ```
13721: 
13722: The switch discriminates on the **top byte only**, so the domain is 256 values, two are
13723: implemented, and **254 of 256 end the host process.**
13724: 
13725: **Reached by two ordinary stores:** write the op into `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` — nothing filters it,
13726: the `case` in the register switch only `debug()`s — then write any DE register with bit `0x800`
13727: set, which makes the handler's tail run `if (startFlag) draw_primitive(cpu, d);`.
13728: 
13729: > **AND IT IS NOT 254 GARBAGE VALUES.** `crmfbreg.h` defines FOUR primitive types —
13730: > `DE_PRIM_POINT` 0x00000000, `DE_PRIM_LINE` 0x01000000, `DE_PRIM_TRIANGLE` 0x02000000,
13731: > `DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE` 0x03000000. The model implements **two**. So a **correct driver drawing a
13732: > POINT or a TRIANGLE — documented hardware, well-formed value — kills the host.** That needs no
13733: > malformed input at all.
13734: 
13735: **`DE_PRIM_POINT` is `0x00000000`, which is also the reset value** (`memset(d, 0, sizeof ...)` at
13736: construction). The register's power-on state names a documented primitive the model dies on.
13737: 
13738: **That coincidence settles a detector disagreement between two seats.** fable5 held a survival
13739: oracle cannot see a spurious `draw_primitive` (the host stays alive); kimi held that against a
13740: pristine register file it dies in `default:` and *is* catchable. **Both are right, conditioned on
13741: register state** — at reset the top byte is POINT, so the draw kills and a startFlagged survival
13742: row catches it; with LINE or RECTANGLE programmed it draws silently and survival sees nothing. A
13743: detector must pin the state it relies on, or the row's channel varies with what the guest did
13744: before it.
13745: 
13746: **It also raises the stakes on `sgidewidth`.** That round must not copy `#497`'s splice because
13747: this tail draws — and the thing it would draw with is lethal on 254 of 256 ops. A naive splice
13748: does not merely cause a spurious primitive; it gives a *declined* access a near-certain host kill.
13749: 
13750: **Not covered by `sgirekills`**, which scopes itself to terminating calls *inside a
13751: `DEVICE_ACCESS`*; this one is in a plain function and sits in the census's other bucket.
13752: 
13753: *Found by reading the kimi seat's 133 KB answer, whose verdict line was the literal words
13754: "verdict line first." — a format miss `seat_verdicts.py` files as "answered, no verdict line",
13755: and which I nearly recorded as a non-answer. No other seat engaged with `draw_primitive`'s default
13756: arm. Third instance this session where the body carried what the verdict line did not.*
13757: 
13758: *No rung-3 witness yet: the reachability above is **read**, not booted. The lifecycle ruling wants
13759: the attempt before this is recorded as unwitnessed.*
