700: 
701: each on the `else` of a `writeflag == MEM_WRITE` test, inside a window whose WRITE path is fully
702: modelled.  **A guest could store to `CRIME_RE_TLB_A` and reading the same address back ended the
703: emulator** -- one `ld` on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.
704: 
705: **FIVE BECAME ACCEPT** -- option-set member 4, because the write arm directly above each one already
706: computes and stores the value, so nothing is unimplemented and a decline would have shipped a wrong
707: record in product code.  **`CRIME_RE_TEX` became a latched DECLINE** on its own bit: `sgitexindex`
708: records its write index as a missing factor of four, only 31 of 112 cells are reachable, `re_tex`
709: has NO consumer in the file, and that row carries a standing do-not-code ruling -- so accepting the
710: read would publish a mapping the project has already recorded as wrong.
711: 
712: **THE FIX HAD TO WRITE `odata`, NOT `data[]`.**  The common tail is
713: `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memory_writemax64(cpu, data, len, odata);`, so a read arm filling
714: `data[]` is overwritten before the guest sees it.  A first draft did exactly that and **would have
715: returned zeroes while looking correct**; it is now mutant `M2_datafill`.
716: 
717: ## THE ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDERING, run rather than asserted
718: 

