381: row("fill alive", "alive" if alive else "died", "alive",
382:     "CONTROL: the host survives a tiled 32-bit fill")
383: #  *** AN INEQUALITY AGAINST THE PAINT PASSES ON EVERY NON-OUTCOME. ***  0x00 (paint never
384: #  landed), 0xde (the `lw' never ran, v0 still SENTINEL), "absent" (the print regex missed) and
385: #  "died" (gxemul never started) are all != 0x99, so the first draft scored them all GREEN.
386: #  Stated POSITIVELY instead: dest[0] must be a byte the fill can actually produce, so each of
387: #  those four is a FAILURE that names itself in the got column.
388: produced = got in ("0x%02x" % CI_TOP, "0x%02x" % RGBA_TOP)
389: row("fill ran", "yes" if produced else got, "yes",
390:     "CONTROL: dest[0] is 0x%02x (CI) or 0x%02x (RGBA) -- not paint/zero/sentinel/died"
391:     % (CI_TOP, RGBA_TOP))
392: #  *** THE DEFECT ROW.  It asserts the CORRECT byte and is expected to FAIL on HEAD. ***
393: row("fill top", got, "0x%02x" % RGBA_TOP,
394:     "dest[0] must be bg>>24 (RGBA), not (uint8_t)bg = 0x%02x (CI)" % CI_TOP)
395: 
396: #  *** `fill top' CANNOT TELL RGBA FROM RGB, AND THAT MUTANT PASSES EVERY OTHER ROW HERE. ***
397: #  Found independently by two seats.  The RGB PUT arm writes {bg>>24, bg>>16, bg>>8, 0x00} --
398: #  IDENTICAL to RGBA at dest[0], different at dest[3].  So a one-token `|= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB'
399: #  substitution is invisible to dest[0] no matter how many fill rows exist.
400: #
401: #  The tail is assertable only once the fix is in, because CI leaves dest[1..3] as host stack.
402: #  So this row is GATED on the leading byte proving the RGBA arm ran: on HEAD it reports `n/a'
403: #  and FAILS -- deterministically, naming its own reason -- instead of scoring a nondeterministic
404: #  stack byte.  Honest in all three states:
405: #      HEAD (CI)      n/a: dest[0] not RGBA   FAIL
406: #      fixed (RGBA)   0x44                    ok
407: #      fixed + RGB    0x00                    FAIL
408: #  `printed()' returns the FIRST match, which is what is wanted here: `got' and `fullword' are
409: #  deliberately the SAME sample, read at two byte positions.
410: #
411: #  *** THE GATE IS ONE SHARED FLAG COMPUTED FROM THE **FILL** SESSION, NOT A PER-ROW TEST. ***
412: #  It is named `fill_top_ok' and not `rgba_ran' on the pass-2 codex seat's correction: fill
413: #  dest[0] == 0x11 is produced by the RGB arm as well as by RGBA, so the flag means "the fill PUT
414: #  put bg>>24 at dest[0]" -- NOT "RGBA specifically ran".  `fill bot' is the row that separates
415: #  those two.  A flag whose name overclaims is how a future reader builds on a fact nobody has.
416: #  The pass-2 glm seat derived this from the packet alone -- under a per-row reading the copy
417: #  rows could never pass, since their own dest[0] is 0x1a and not 0x11, so BASE 13/13 would be
418: #  impossible.  It is a FIX-PRESENCE indicator: it says the repaired branch fired at all, which
419: #  is the precondition for any tail byte being defined.  A copy row therefore reports n/a when
420: #  the FILL was CI, even if the copy path itself was fine -- see the message text below.
421: #
422: #  *** TWO MEASURED LIMITS OF THIS PROXY, both from the pass-2 grok seat, recorded rather than
423: #  glossed because they are the kind of thing a future round would otherwise rediscover: ***
424: #
425: #  (a) UNDER A `&& (mode & MTE_MODE_COPY)' MUTANT THE COPY ROWS ARE A WRONG-REASON KILL.  The
426: #      copy path there really does take RGBA -- dest[3] would be 0x4d and would PASS -- but the
427: #      FILL is CI, so the gate closes and both rows red anyway.  Their own bytes do not move.
428: #      That mutant is still killed by `fill top', so nothing escapes; but the CHANNEL those two
